    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests green).\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed owner deletion from a hard delete to a soft delete across the stack:\n\n**1. `Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a persistent `deleted` field (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`, `Boolean`, initialized to `false`) with `getDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`. A newly mapped owner therefore starts `deleted = false`.\n\n**2. Database schema** (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`) \u2014 added a `deleted` column to the `owners` table with `DEFAULT FALSE` (`DEFAULT 0` for MySQL's `BIT`), so existing seed rows load as non-deleted. The tests run on the `hsqldb` profile (`ddl-auto=none`), so the schema change is what actually creates the column.\n\n**3. OpenAPI spec** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema. DTOs are generated at build time, so this produces `OwnerDto.getDeleted()/setDeleted()`, which MapStruct auto-maps from the entity. That's what makes `GET /api/owners/{id}` return `\"deleted\": false`/`true`.\n\n**4. `OwnerRestControllerV1`**:\n- `deleteOwner` now sets `owner.setDeleted(true)` and calls `saveOwner(...)` instead of removing the row, still returning `204` (2xx). `findOwnerById` (unfiltered) keeps returning the row afterward, so GET still shows the owner with `deleted = true`.\n- `existingOwners()` \u2014 the single scan point feeding the duplicate, identity, and household checks \u2014 now filters out owners flagged deleted, so a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only match has been soft-deleted.\n\nThe two acceptance tests (`coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains` and `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck`) both pass.\n\nOne note: I scoped the \"ignore deleted\" filter at `existingOwners()`, which means the create-time count/quota rules (namesake count, city capacity, customer-code uniqueness, etc.) also skip deleted owners \u2014 not just duplicate/identity. That's consistent with treating a deleted owner as absent from create-time logic, and no test depends on the narrower interpretation, but let me know if you'd want deleted owners excluded *only* from the duplicate and identity checks.",
